\section{Scaling, and why it is off}{
\code{center} and \code{scale} are \code{FALSE} here and \code{TRUE} in \code{\link[=perform_pca]{perform_pca()}} and
\code{\link[=perform_tsne]{perform_tsne()}}. The difference is \code{metric}: \code{"cosine"} and \code{"pearson"} compare
the shape of a row rather than its size, so they have already answered the
question standardising would answer, and the \code{umap} package does not standardise
either. The default is therefore the engine's own picture of the data as it
arrived.

What that leaves the caller is one decision rather than none. With
\code{metric = "euclidean"} or \code{"manhattan"} on features that are not measured on a
common scale, the feature with the widest units decides who is whose neighbour,
and \code{scale = TRUE} is what the picture needs — it is also what makes this
embedding comparable with a \code{\link[=perform_pca]{perform_pca()}} or \code{\link[=perform_tsne]{perform_tsne()}} of the same data,
since those two standardise by default.

One consequence of the default is that a feature of no variance is kept. It can
be kept because nothing divides by its standard deviation; it contributes nothing
to any distance either way. With \code{scale = TRUE} it is left out with a message and
named in \code{design$dropped_feats}.
}

\section{Choosing the margin}{
\code{embedding_scale = "features"} embeds the features instead, and unlike
\code{\link[=perform_pca]{perform_pca()}} this really does transpose: UMAP embeds the rows it is handed and
has no second answer to read off the same fit. \code{center} and \code{scale}, if they are
turned on, still apply to the \strong{features} and the transpose is then embedded as
it stands, which is what makes this the same margin
\code{perform_pca(embedding_scale = "features")} reports on. \code{perform_umap(t(data))}
with \code{scale = TRUE} would standardise samples instead, which is a third analysis.

A feature margin needs enough features to be worth drawing. \code{n_neighbors} is read
off the number of points, so 8 features force a neighbourhood of 8 and a message
says so; at that size the loadings of a PCA are the whole answer.
}

\section{What is dropped before anything runs}{
\code{umap} does not accept a missing value, so rows that are not complete and finite
across \code{feats} are dropped before it is called, and \code{design$n_dropped} reports how
many went. This is the listwise deletion the rest of the package uses; nothing is
imputed.
